  body: `Prior Lessons — 1-Sentence Recap

Lesson 044: Collective operations share or combine accelerator results, so slow communication can leave the whole group waiting.

Lesson 045: Network interfaces move data out of servers, while switches choose shared paths that can become bottlenecks.

Lesson 046: Copper is efficient over short connections, while optics adds hardware and power to carry signals reliably over longer distances.

Lesson 047: Synchronized workers depend on one another, so one slow or failed participant can waste time across an expensive cluster.

Lesson 048: Adding accelerators helps only when the workload uses them well enough to justify the extra communication, waiting and cost.

The GPUs arrived. Why can’t we turn them on?

Imagine four new AI servers arriving at a data center. There is space in the rack, the network is ready, and the building has unused electricity. Yet the operator cannot run all four at full power.

The problem is the final delivery path: the rack’s electrical equipment cannot carry their combined demand. This is an illustrative deployment, but it exposes a real distinction. Buying compute and making compute usable are separate achievements.

After learning how accelerators communicate, we now follow what lets them operate at all: electricity. By the end, you should be able to distinguish a shortage of energy from a limit on power delivery—and explain why either can matter to AI economics.

A rack is also an electrical commitment

NVIDIA specifies up to 14.3 kilowatts for a DGX B200 system containing eight GPUs. Four such systems would have a combined maximum specification of 57.2 kW, before additional rack networking. That is our planning example, not a measured operating load or a complete rack design.

A kilowatt is 1,000 watts. It measures power: the rate at which electricity is used. A kilowatt-hour measures energy over time. A rack drawing 50 kW steadily for one hour uses 50 kWh.

That distinction separates two questions: can the equipment carry the load at this moment, and how much electricity will we pay for over time? A cheap electricity contract does not increase the capacity of a cable.

Maximum specifications help size infrastructure. Actual demand changes with the workload; measured peaks and averages answer different questions. Planning from the average alone can leave the system unable to handle a higher load.

Follow the last few metres

The following simplified path shows conventional rack distribution. Some systems use different arrangements, such as shared power shelves, but every design must distribute and convert electricity before the chips can use it.

Building circuit — brings power to the rack

↓

Rack PDU — distributes it to equipment

↓

Server PSU — converts incoming AC to DC

↓

Board regulators — supply the chips’ low DC voltages

A rack power distribution unit, or PDU, provides appropriately rated connections for the equipment and may monitor individual outlets. Cords carry power onward. A server power supply unit, or PSU, converts alternating current (AC) into direct current (DC). AC changes direction periodically; electronic components need controlled DC supplies.

The conversion does not end at the server entrance. Circuits on the boards reduce the voltage further for GPUs, CPUs, memory and networking. Each conversion loses some energy as heat, so the electricity entering a server exceeds the electricity delivered to its chips.

Return to our four servers. If their rack PDU or upstream circuit is the constraint, spare building capacity cannot bypass it. Installing a larger PDU alone also fails if the wiring feeding it remains undersized. The entire required path must support the load.

